# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <setjmp.h>

typedef long jmp_buf[32];
typedef long sigjmp_buf[32];

int setjmp(jmp_buf env);
int _setjmp(jmp_buf env);
void longjmp(jmp_buf env, int value);
void _longjmp(jmp_buf env, int value);
#define sigsetjmp(env, save) setjmp(env)
#define siglongjmp(env, value) longjmp(env, value)
```

# DESCRIPTION

`setjmp` saves the calling function's callee-saved registers, its stack pointer and its return address in <env> and returns 0.
`longjmp` restores them, so that the `setjmp` returns again, with <value>, or 1 if <value> is 0.
The function that called `setjmp` must not have returned.
As C has it, a local variable changed between the two and not declared `volatile` has an indeterminate value after the jump.

The registers saved are each psABI's callee-saved ones: on arm64 x19 to x30, the stack pointer, and d8 to d15; on amd64 rbx, rbp, r12 to r15, the stack pointer and the return address; on rv64 ra, sp, s0 to s11, and fs0 to fs11 under a hard-float ABI (user code is soft-float, so none there).

`_setjmp` and `_longjmp` are the same calls, and so are `sigsetjmp` and `siglongjmp`: there are no signals, so no signal mask to keep.

